1 RENAME

   Changes the names of files on a mounted volume, or moves them to
   another directory on it.  The files' contents aren't copied.

   Format

     RENAME input-filespec[,...] output-filespec

2 Parameters

 input-filespec[,...]

   The files to rename.  Wildcards are allowed.  Without a version, each
   name's newest version is renamed.  The device and directory of one
   input are the defaults of the next, and the parts left out of the
   first are the default device and directory's (see SET DEFAULT).

 output-filespec

   The new name.  A part left out (device, directory, name, or type) is
   the input file's own, and a name, type, or version written as * is
   the input file's too, so one output serves several files (RENAME
   *.TXT *.OLD).  No other wildcards are allowed.

   With no version in the output, a file keeps its version if the input
   named one (or ;*), and otherwise gets the next version of its new
   name.  An existing name and version is never replaced.

2 Description

   A file can't be moved to another volume: the input and output must be
   on the same one.  A directory file (NAME.DIR;1) can be renamed or
   moved like any other file, and everything in it goes along, but not
   into itself or one of its own subdirectories.  A file that can't be
   renamed is reported, and the rest are still renamed.

2 Qualifiers
/LOG

   Shows each file renamed.

/[NO]NEW_VERSION

   With no versions given, /NEW_VERSION (the default) gives each file
   the next version of its new name, and /NONEW_VERSION has each keep
   its version.

2 Examples

   1.$ RENAME FEES.DAT;4 [SAVETEXT]RECORDS.DAT

     Moves FEES.DAT;4 to [SAVETEXT] as RECORDS.DAT;4.

   2.$ RENAME/LOG *.TXT *.OLD

     Gives each .TXT file the type .OLD, showing each.

   3.$ RENAME [A]X.TXT,Y.TXT [B]

     Moves X.TXT and Y.TXT from [A] to [B].
